     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\":2,\"rationale\":\"Blocking h1_2:escalate and h1_1:access makes crown:obtain unreachable, and no single block can, so 2 is the minimum. With h1_2 blocked, h3_2 is unreachable because h1_2 is its only parent. h2_1 is also unreachable, since it is an AND that needs h1_2, so h3_1 is unreachable too. h4_1 then has no reachable parent, which cuts off h5_0. h5_2 is an AND that needs h3_2, so it is cut off as well. That leaves only h5_1 via h3_0, and h3_0 depends solely on h1_1, so blocking h1_1 removes it. One block is not enough because two attack chains share no blockable step: edge0/edge1 -> h1_1 -> h3_0 -> h5_1 -> crown, and edge1 -> h1_2 -> h3_2 -> h5_1 -> crown. They meet only at h5_1, and blocking h5_1 alone still leaves crown reachable via h1_2 -> h3_2 -> h4_1 -> h5_0.\"}"
    }
   ],
